Q: How do I prepend file to beginning? A: <p>Technically there is <strong>no way</strong> to append a file at the top. All you can do and all other answers implement, is to write into a completely new file and then copy entire contents of the old file, then delete it.</p>
<p><a href="https://www.php.net/manual/en/function.stream-copy-to-stream.php" rel="nofollow noreferrer">stream_copy_to_stream()</a> looks fit for the task</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>function file_write_atop($filename, $add) {
    $tmp = tempnam(sys_get_temp_dir(), __FUNCTION__);
    $th = fopen($tmp, 'w');
    fwrite($th, $add);

    $oh = fopen($filename, 'r');
    stream_copy_to_stream($oh, $th);
    fclose($th);
    fclose($oh);
    rename($tmp, $filename);
}

</code></pre>
<p>Peak memory usage recorded using a code like this</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>echo &quot;memory usage: &quot;, (round(memory_get_usage() / 1024)), &quot;K\n&quot;;
file_write_atop('test.txt', &quot;string to prepend\n&quot;);
echo &quot;peak memory usage: &quot;, (round(memory_get_peak_usage() / 1024)), &quot;K\n&quot;;
</code></pre>
<p>recorded 35K usage for a 100M file.</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Warning:</strong> this approach only fits for a one-time task but by no means for a constant writing such as logs. For a log, append it at the bottom the usual way  and only reverse it on display.</p>
</blockquote>
